WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.900
Take a circle, and a piece of string
exactly as long as its radius.

2
00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:07.030
Lay the string around the edge.

3
00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:10.360
The angle it makes at
the centre is one radian.

4
00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:13.130
Keep laying radius-lengths
round the circle.

5
00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:17.930
One, two, three, up to
six, with a small gap left.

6
00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:22.210
But degrees already work: three
hundred and sixty in a full turn.

7
00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:23.700
So why radians?

8
00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:25.050
Are they more accurate?

9
00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:26.860
Do calculators need them?

10
00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:29.720
In this mission, you'll define one radian:

11
00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:33.510
the angle at the centre when the
arc length equals the radius.

12
00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:36.830
You'll see that a full
turn is two pi radians.

13
00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:41.270
And you'll find arc lengths and sector
areas from the radius and the angle.

14
00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:46.680
Your mission: explain why mathematicians
measure angles in radians, not degrees.
